msgid "Archived: Quick Start Guide for Ready-to-Fly Vehicles"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/quick-start-guide.rst:9
#: bfb3d3822a234e89994a82522aba9125
msgid "**ARCHIVED ARTICLE**"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/quick-start-guide.rst:11
#: bea66471b3074c4e8d647c2f83523f62
msgid "The article is out of date (3DR replaced APM2.x with Pixhawk and the kits come with everything configured out of the box). In addition, 3DR no longer supplies hardware described in this article (including Iris+, Solo, X8, Aero-M)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/quick-start-guide.rst:15
#: d431579e84f24de6a1b6d88063dcee2b
msgid "This page is a quick start guide for users who have purchased a *3DRobotics Ready-to-Fly (RTF) multicopter* including a Pixhawk preloaded with the latest Copter firmware. All other users should skip to the :ref:`First Time Setup <initial-setup>` section which includes the full autopilot set-up and calibration instructions."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/quick-start-guide.rst:21
#: e3b6c941aca4489e8d4d9fb5dbb89755
msgid "Install Mission Planner"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/quick-start-guide.rst:23
#: 07ca6567b6824b4aaaecd611dae5e93d
msgid "`Download the most recent Mission Planner MSI from here <https://firmware.ardupilot.org/Tools/MissionPlanner/MissionPlanner-latest.msi>`__."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/quick-start-guide.rst:25
#: ba584752368549ce928ab0408b77cc49
msgid "Open the Microsoft installer file (.msi) and select **Run** to run the installation utility."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/quick-start-guide.rst:31
#: 92e38e5a55c84c7788a84bafb20997d0
msgid "Follow the instructions to complete the setup process (accept the license terms, choose a location for the installation etc.)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/quick-start-guide.rst:34
#: e179b22920a045f895e716a92fd1f082
msgid "The installation utility will automatically install any necessary software drivers. If you receive a DirectX installation error, please update your DirectX plug-in from the `Windows Download Center <https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/download/windows.aspx>`__. If you receive the warning pictured here, select **Install this driver software anyway** to continue."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/quick-start-guide.rst:41
#: febc352853834662a3e5d62d77b1971d
msgid "Once installation is complete, open Mission Planner. Mission Planner automatically notifies you about available updates. Please always run the most current version of Mission Planner."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/quick-start-guide.rst:46
#: 15357e3bd53b4499b97dd3178eddb4dc
msgid "Connect RC receiver"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/quick-start-guide.rst:48
#: fc7287d7a21044a6946e25925343d240
msgid "Is your RC receiver already connected and configured? If not, follow these instructions."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/quick-start-guide.rst:51
#: cbad0468c14146ac9dd2a9e10404707c
msgid "For RC control with \\ **PWM**, connect RC receiver to APM Input Signal pins using connectors to attach channel 1 to the APM signal pin labeled 1, channel 2 to signal pin 2, etc. You will also need to provide power to the receiver by connecting a three-wire cable between the receiver and the APM for one of the channels."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/quick-start-guide.rst:60
#: 823d4fac1186433196cd00e0d60a79fb
msgid "For **PPM** (all channels over one wire), use PPM jumper connectors to connect Input signal pins 2 and 3 on APM and signal pins 3 and 4 on the receiver. Use a 3-wire cable to connect the signal, power, and ground pins for channel 1 on the receiver to the three pins labeled 1 on APM. The specific pins used for the receiver may vary by model, so consult the documentation on your receiver for PPM configuration."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/quick-start-guide.rst:70
#: 0b6209ac1d054de6b481a81076813da5
msgid "Once you've downloaded Mission Planner onto your ground station computer, connect APM to your computer using the micro USB connector and APM's micro USB port. Use a direct USB port on your computer, not a USB hub."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/quick-start-guide.rst:78
#: be5424d1859a48148b264b2d3eb76d26
msgid "Windows will automatically detect APM and install the correct driver software."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/quick-start-guide.rst:81
#: 14c9fc1368084cdbaf7aa30579c88e4e
msgid "Next we'll let Mission Planner know which port we're using to connect to APM. Open Mission Planner, and use the drop-down menus in the upper-right corner of the screen (near the Connect button) to connect to APM. Select \\ **Arduino Mega 2560** and set the Baud rate to **115200** as shown."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/quick-start-guide.rst:90
#: 969aef2277b74364aa509d41a7c763cc
msgid "Select \\ **Connect** (upper-right corner of the screen) to load MavLink parameters to APM. Mission Planner will display a window showing the progress of the MavLink download."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/quick-start-guide.rst:97
#: 36ab06b1cf4b4505b07e8ca674ed3166
msgid "When the window displays **Done** and Mission Planner shows the **Disconnect** option in place of Connect, your APM firmware has been downloaded successfully."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/quick-start-guide.rst:104
#: 19e72b3d24bb49a6a46ab40fe3e39788
msgid "Turn on transmitter. Verify that the transmitter is in Airplane mode (APM needs airplane mode regardless of the platform type being piloted) and all trims are centered."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/quick-start-guide.rst:108
#: 33c3da2f1f924b65aa55c2451e581b5a
msgid "Transmitter(Mode 2): Recommended Channels"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/quick-start-guide.rst:110
#: ca1f203f72bc46ad86d7fb210bfecaf3
msgid "The left stick will control throttle and yaw; the right stick will control roll and pitch. The three-position switch will control flight modes."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/quick-start-guide.rst:114
#: cab9b313ed514c39b21758c6fe95cf9e
msgid "In Mission Planner, select **Mandatory Hardware** and **Radio Calibration**. Click on the green \\ **Calibrate Radio** button in the lower right of the window. Mission Planner will call a dialog window to ensure radio control equipment is on, battery is not connected, and propellers are not attached."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/quick-start-guide.rst:123
#: 8b40c700de024cf9b66429e521b0c64a
msgid "Select OK; move the control sticks and toggle switches on your transmitter to their limits of travel and observe the results on the radio calibration bars. Red lines will appear across the calibration bars to indicate maximum and minimum values. Move the Ch 5 and 6 toggle switches through their range of positions (Ch 7 and 8 are not used for basic operations)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/quick-start-guide.rst:130
#: 61a721a81491428599566801f9bd1816
msgid "Your transmitter should cause the following control changes:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/quick-start-guide.rst:132
#: ff10e469541d47a68515f896bac64da5
msgid "Channel 1: low = roll left, high = roll right."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/quick-start-guide.rst:134
#: ac29c86fd6f14cd3b3c318e072d5c667
msgid "Channel 2: low = pitch forward, high=pitch back."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/quick-start-guide.rst:136
#: c6c490a11e564b6a98590a8019bffbe6
msgid "Channel 3: low = throttle down (off), high = throttle up."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/quick-start-guide.rst:138
#: 338666c62cde4f0abcfc6f5d605773bc
msgid "Channel 4: low = yaw left, high = yaw right."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/quick-start-guide.rst:143
#: f4e91cbbfe0b4dfe9b5e5b10754cff93
msgid "When the red bars for roll, pitch, throttle, yaw, and radio 5 (and optionally radio 6) are set at the minimum and maximum positions, select **Click when Done**. Mission Planner will show a summary of the calibration data. Normal values are around 1100 for minimums and 1900 for maximums."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/quick-start-guide.rst:153
#: 62d1ecc28fa247a58fe6315b3b7714b5
msgid "Attach propellers"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/quick-start-guide.rst:155
#: 9838dbc08f8b4b3b8e8cd5225a93db08
msgid "Unscrew prop nuts and add propellers to motor collets with writing on props facing up in relation to the sky. If you have a co-axial motor configuration, make sure that the writing on the props faces up even if the motor faces down. Add metal spacers on top of prop nuts if prop hub measures less than 5 mm in height. Tighten prop nuts to secure propellers in place. Try inserting a 2 mm (5/64) hex wrench into the hole in the side of the prop nut to get better leverage when tightening prop nuts. Select propellers based on motor directions. Find your frame in the images below. Use pusher propellers for motors marked clockwise and normal propellers marked counterclockwise. Pusher propellers are usually marked \"P\" or \"SFP\" on the propeller."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/quick-start-guide.rst:177
#: 02a35e8adfe04db38ff6bca8d23df982
msgid "Pre-flight information"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/quick-start-guide.rst:179
#: a83445aa08c146da96027d0fa2dbebdd
msgid "Safety is the key to successful flight. Please read the \\ :ref:`Safety Information Page <safety-multicopter>` before flying. Select an unpopulated area to fly your copter, away from people and developed areas, and always run the :ref:`pre-flight checklist <checklist>`\\ before connecting the battery. Make sure to read the :ref:`Flying <flying-arducopter>` page for information about arming, disarming, and controlling your copter during flight."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/quick-start-guide.rst:185
#: 2a4afee2e7a84888888ea172608281fa
msgid "When you're ready to fly, place copter on the ground facing away from you. Connect fully charged LiPo battery to the power module connector when you're ready to power on your copter. Follow the safe arming and disarming procedure described on the :ref:`Maiden Flight <flying-arducopter>` page."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/quick-start-guide.rst:194
#: 30adeded53484bf7a5775463a676ef06
msgid "Next steps"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/quick-start-guide.rst:196
#: 720d1b1594e948719e69b032cfcc122b
msgid "Try learning about different :ref:`autonomous flight modes <flight-modes>` to get the most out of your Copter, including loitering and running missions."
msgstr ""
